/// Enumerable(E) — iteration and query methods derived from one required
/// snapshot: `elements -> List(E)` (ADR 0127 §9).
///
/// Every provision forwards to the matching `List` primitive over that
/// snapshot — no block ever crosses a self-send. This is deliberate, not
/// an optimisation: a block passed to a provided method that itself called
/// another provision through a self-send (`do:` written in terms of a
/// required `do:`, `size` as `self inject: 0 into: …`) loses `^` and
/// captured-local writes when the using class is an `Actor` (BT-3580) — the
/// block runs in the actor's process, and the NLR throw and any captured
/// write never make it back out through the self-send. Forwarding to
/// `elements` (a plain value, already snapshotted on the caller's side)
/// sidesteps the bug entirely: every block below runs in the *caller's*
/// process, exactly as sending it straight to a `List` would.
///
/// `Collection` does **not** `uses:` this protocol — its own `select:`/
/// `collect:` already answer `Self` via the species pattern, richer than
/// this protocol's plain `List(E)` answers. But `Collection` does gain
/// `elements -> List(E) => self asList` (structural conformance only), so
/// every `List`, `Set`, `Array`, … conforms to `Enumerable` without needing
/// `uses:` — see `stdlib/src/collection.bt`.
///
/// ## Examples
/// ```beamtalk
/// pool elements select: [:w | w isIdle]    // caller-side, safe even for an Actor
/// pool size                                // => pool elements size
/// ```
Protocol define: Enumerable(E)
  /// Required — the elements, in the receiver's order, as a List snapshot.
  elements -> List(E)

  /// The number of elements.
  size -> Integer => self elements size

  /// True if there are no elements.
  isEmpty -> Boolean => self elements isEmpty

  /// True if there is at least one element.
  isNotEmpty -> Boolean => self elements isNotEmpty

  /// Iterate over each element, evaluating `block` with each one.
  do: block :: Block(E, Object) -> Nil => self elements do: block

  /// Reduce the elements with an accumulator, evaluating `block` with
  /// `(accumulator, element)` for each one.
  inject: initial :: A into: block :: Block(A, E, A) -> A =>
    self elements inject: initial into: block

  /// Return the elements for which `block` answers `true`.
  select: block :: Block(E, Boolean) -> List(E) => self elements select: block

  /// Collect the result of evaluating `block` on each element.
  collect: block :: Block(E, R) -> List(R) => self elements collect: block

  /// Find the first element matching `block`, or evaluate `noneBlock` if
  /// none match.
  detect: block :: Block(E, Boolean) ifNone: noneBlock :: Block(T) -> E | T =>
    self elements detect: block ifNone: noneBlock

  /// True if any element satisfies `block`.
  anySatisfy: block :: Block(E, Boolean) -> Boolean =>
    self elements anySatisfy: block

  /// Count the elements for which `block` answers `true`.
  count: block :: Block(E, Boolean) -> Integer => self elements count: block
